<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Queen Soraya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Shah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>, whom she married in 1951. The daughter of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> noble and a German mother, she was divorced in 1958 because the marriage had produced no heir. She then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, briefly acting in films, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soraya-esfandiary-bakhtiary.jpg" alt="Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soraya_Esfandiary-Bakhtiari-045.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Soraya was born on 22 June 1932 at the English Missionary Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, the elder of two children and the only daughter of Khalil Esfandiary-Bakhtiary, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> nobleman who became Iranian ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in the 1950s, and his Russian-born German wife Eva Karl. Her family had long served in the Iranian government and diplomatic corps, and a great-uncle, Sardar Assad, was a leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution</a>. She was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and Isfahan and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>In 1948 a relative introduced her to the recently divorced Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</a> by means of a photograph taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and they were soon engaged. She married the Shah at the Marble Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> on 12 February 1951, in a wedding with 2,000 guests and a gown designed by Christian Dior. Her upbringing had been German and Catholic, and some Muslim clerics objected to a half-European girl who had not been raised a Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As queen she headed the family charity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In August 1953 she followed the Shah to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> when he fled during the confrontation with Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, and he returned after the coup that overthrew Mosaddegh. In 1954 the Shah, concerned that she had not given him an heir, took her to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to consult doctors, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> she was told she was infertile. A photograph of her water-skiing in a bikini at Miami caused controversy and was banned in Iran.</p><p>The marriage disintegrated by early 1958. The Shah's mother pressed him to divorce her, and Soraya refused to accept that he take a second wife. The divorce was announced in March 1958 and the marriage ended officially on 6 April. She received a penthouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and a monthly allowance. Afterwards she briefly acted in films, using her first name only, and starred in I tre volti in 1965 and became the companion of its director, Franco Indovina, who died in a plane crash in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Soraya wrote two memoirs, one published in 1964 and Le Palais des solitudes in 1991, in which she described her depression. She died on 25 October 2001 in her apartment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, aged 69, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> beside her parents and brother. Her belongings were sold at auction in Paris in 2002 for more than 8.3 million dollars, and a dispute over her estate continued until 2016. Her divorce inspired the song Je veux pleurer comme Soraya, released in 1995.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bakhtiari remain a distinct people; <b>Queen Soraya</b> of Pahlavi Iran was of Bakhtiari descent.</blockquote><a href="/state/bakhtiari-confederation">Bakhtiari confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Isa ibn Maryam Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.67&amp;year=1976" aria-label="Isa ibn Maryam Hospital on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.67&amp;year=1976">What was here in 1976? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
